  "narrative": "Across 100 simulated trials, each with its own independent demand sequence, the baseline (undisrupted) OEM fill rate averaged 89.7%, ranging from 86.5% in the worst 5% of trials to 93.0% in the best 5%. Under the modeled disruption, fill rate averaged 50.4% (p5=45.9%, p95=53.8%). Paired against the same demand seed in each trial (so the comparison isolates the disruption's own effect from ordinary demand noise), the disruption caused a drop in fill rate averaging 39.3% -- a severe impact -- with the 5th-to-95th percentile range spanning -41.7% to -36.1% across trials. This effect was directionally consistent across nearly all trials -- even the most favorable 5% of trials still showed an impact in the same direction. Final-week backlog fell by 2,009,463 kg LCE on average (p5=-3,298,070, p95=-665,676 kg LCE). During the disruption's active window and its immediate aftermath, the tracked metric changed by -2,496,904 on average -- this transient, window-level view can surface an effect that fully washes out by the run's final week and wouldn't otherwise show up in the final-week numbers above.",
